    "answer": {
      "short": "The congruent pairs are:\n\n$y=x^2$ and $y=x^2-x$;\n\n$y=-2x^2$ and $y=3-2x^2$;\n\n$y=3x^2$ and $y=3x^2+1$;\n\n$y=2+3x-4x^2$ and $y=1+4x^2$.\n\nTranslations and reflections preserve shape; each pair has the same magnitude of the coefficient of $x^2$.",
      "worked": "Translations and reflections preserve congruence. Completing the square shows that the linear terms change the vertex, but not the shape:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-x&=\\left(x-\\frac12\\right)^2-\\frac14\\\\2+3x-4x^2&=-4\\left(x-\\frac38\\right)^2+\\frac{41}{16}\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe magnitudes of the coefficients of the squared terms are therefore $1,2,3,3,4,2,1,4$, in the printed order. Matching equal magnitudes gives:\n\n$y=x^2$ and $y=x^2-x$;\n\n$y=-2x^2$ and $y=3-2x^2$;\n\n$y=3x^2$ and $y=3x^2+1$;\n\n$y=2+3x-4x^2$ and $y=1+4x^2$.\n\nThe last pair also requires a reflection because the parabolas open in opposite directions.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
